the throng of cities the matrix of  modern life is propped up by people  whose work is insecure  but in the shadows there's exploitation  and it's gotten worse  shortly after dawn ivan commutes to  glassy west london originally from  ecuador he's a cleaner and paid below  the london living wage  since the pandemic when some staff were  let go he claims his workload has  doubled for no extra pay he was harassed  if he took a break and wasn't even  allowed to leave the building while on  shift  stories like his are often obscured but  not unusual in fact nearly half of  precarious workers surveyed by their  unions said they'd had pay withheld at  some point in the year to july  a quarter were forced to accept worse  terms one in five were too scared to  take time off sick and there are also  more reports of sexual harassment  early lockdowns and job losses left  workers with few alternatives and  therefore fewer choices  recent labor shortages might imply that  there are more options for workers more  bargaining power but for the most  vulnerable that simply isn't the case  the pandemics offered some employers the  excuse to embed exploitative practices  and for those who simply can't afford to  take even a week unpaid or who don't  speak english the risks are simply too  high this is compounded say experts by  inadequate safety nets very low  statutory sick pay and delays in benefit  payouts when the system allows core  behavior poor behavior happens there are  good secure jobs at the top and then you  have this other class of workers  essentially who don't have those  protections who are low paid they don't  have protection against unfair dismissal  they don't get redundancy pay  they don't have proper sick pay  as the winter and spiraling cost of  living sets in the fear of fighting back  will entrench  times may be tight for employers but the  unseen and the vulnerable should not  bear the brunt helen smith sky news
 

